---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/url.js';

const checks = [
  {
    place: 'A normal street or garden',
    applies: 'Landowner permission for take-off and landing',
    lead: 'None',
  },
  {
    place: 'Inside the Swansea Airport zone',
    applies: 'Airport permission, plus a call to the tower before launch',
    lead: 'A few days',
  },
  {
    place: 'Inside the Cardiff Airport zone',
    applies: 'Air traffic control permission',
    lead: 'A few days',
  },
  {
    place: 'National Trust coast — Rhossili, land around Three Cliffs',
    applies: 'Written permission from the Trust; commercial work goes through their filming office',
    lead: 'Weeks',
  },
  {
    place: 'Bannau Brycheiniog National Park',
    applies: 'Filming enquiry to the park authority, and possibly Natural Resources Wales too',
    lead: 'Weeks',
  },
  {
    place: 'A Cadw monument',
    applies: 'Application form, £5m public liability, method statement and risk assessment',
    lead: 'Weeks',
  },
  {
    place: 'Sennybridge and the Epynt ranges',
    applies: 'Military land inside a danger area',
    lead: 'Not available',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Where I can fly"
  description="Where drone photography is and isn't possible in South Wales: the Swansea and Cardiff airport zones, National Trust land on Gower, Bannau Brycheiniog and Cadw sites."
>
  <article>
    <header class="section head">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">Where I can fly</p>
        <h1>What I check before every job.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Most of the questions I get start with "can you actually fly there?" Usually yes. Sometimes yes with two
          weeks' notice. Occasionally no, and it is better to know that before you book a photographer than after.
        </p>
        <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1rem">
          This is the list I work through for every property, in the order I work through it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="quick">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 id="quick">The short version</h2>
        <div class="tablewrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Where the property is</th>
                <th scope="col">What has to happen first</th>
                <th scope="col">Notice needed</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {
                checks.map((c) => (
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row">{c.place}</th>
                    <td>{c.applies}</td>
                    <td class="mono">{c.lead}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose-section" aria-labelledby="aircraft">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 id="aircraft">Why the aircraft matters more than you'd think</h2>
        <div class="cols">
          <p>
            I fly a sub-250g aircraft in the UK0/C0 class. That puts the work in the Open category, subcategory A1,
            and A1 is the part that decides whether your house can be photographed at all.
          </p>
          <p>
            In A1 I can fly in residential, recreational, commercial and industrial areas, and closer to people
            than fifty metres. A heavier aircraft flying in A3 has to stay a hundred and fifty metres clear of
            those areas, which rules out almost every house on a normal street.
          </p>
          <p>
            The one hard limit that does not go away: no flying over a crowd. The Civil Aviation Authority defines
            that as a group of people who could not move away quickly because of how many others are around them.
            A busy beach on a bank holiday counts.
          </p>
          <p class="src">
            <a href="https://www.caa.co.uk/drones/open-category/drone-code/where-you-can-fly-points-3-to-9" rel="noopener">CAA — where you can fly</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose-section" aria-labelledby="airports">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 id="airports">Airport zones</h2>
        <div class="cols">
          <p>
            Protected aerodromes have a flight restriction zone around them: a circle of two to two and a half
            nautical miles, plus a corridor a kilometre wide running five kilometres out from each end of the
            runway, all the way up to two thousand feet. Inside that, you need the airport's permission before you
            take off. Not a notification. Permission.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Swansea Airport</strong> sits at Fairwood Common, which is on the Gower peninsula rather than
            at the edge of it, so a fair slice of eastern Gower falls inside its zone. They take requests through a
            form on their website and ask for a call to the tower twenty minutes before launch so they can pass a
            warning to other aircraft. In my experience they are straightforward to deal with. Worth knowing that
            they run parachuting at weekends in spring and winter, with descents from fifteen thousand feet, which
            can move a slot.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cardiff Airport</strong> has its own zone covering a good part of the Vale of Glamorgan.
            Permission comes from air traffic control there.
          </p>
          <p class="src">
            <a href="https://swansea-airport.wales/about/drones/" rel="noopener">Swansea Airport — drones</a> ·
            <a href="https://www.caa.co.uk/drones/moving-on-to-more-advanced-flying/airspace/aerodromes-heliports-and-spaceports/" rel="noopener">CAA — aerodromes</a> ·
            <a href="https://nats-uk.ead-it.com/cms-nats/opencms/en/uas-restriction-zones/" rel="noopener">NATS — restriction zones</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose-section" aria-labelledby="nt">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 id="nt">National Trust land, which is most of the Gower coast</h2>
        <div class="cols">
          <p>
            This is the one that catches people out, because the Gower coastline everybody wants photographed is
            largely National Trust land. Rhossili and the ground around Three Cliffs are the obvious examples.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Trust's position is that it does not permit unauthorised drone operation on its land, and its
            byelaws state that an unauthorised person shall not operate an airborne vehicle over National Trust
            property. Whatever view you take of how far that reaches into the airspace above, the part nobody
            argues about is the ground: taking off or landing on their land without permission is not on.
          </p>
          <p>
            For paid work they route it through their filming and locations office, which means an application,
            evidence of competence and insurance, location fees, and footage licensed only for the project it was
            shot for. It is a real process with a real lead time, so if your let is on Trust land, tell me early.
          </p>
          <p class="src">
            <a href="https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/who-we-are/about-us/flying-drones-at-our-places" rel="noopener">National Trust — flying drones at our places</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose-section" aria-labelledby="park">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 id="park">Bannau Brycheiniog</h2>
        <div class="cols">
          <p>
            The national park authority asks that nothing is filmed on park land without permission, and runs a
            filming enquiry process to grant it. Their published rates are aimed at feature films and advertising;
            anything smaller is negotiated case by case, so a property shoot is a conversation rather than a
            price list.
          </p>
          <p>
            Give it lead time. Depending where the property is, Natural Resources Wales may need to sign off as
            well, and that is not a same-week answer.
          </p>
          <p class="src">
            <a href="https://beacons-npa.gov.uk/the-authority/filming-in-the-park" rel="noopener">Bannau Brycheiniog — filming in the park</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose-section" aria-labelledby="mod">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 id="mod">Sennybridge and the Epynt ranges</h2>
        <div class="cols">
          <p>
            Military training land, sitting inside danger area D203. Live firing, and a permanent restriction
            rather than a seasonal one. I do not fly there and I would be sceptical of anyone offering to.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose-section" aria-labelledby="cadw">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 id="cadw">Castles and scheduled monuments</h2>
        <div class="cols">
          <p>
            Cadw runs its own policy, separate from everything above. You can fly over a scheduled monument if you
            are complying with the drone code, but Cadw does not allow take-off or landing on its property without
            going through them first.
          </p>
          <p>
            Commercial work means an application form, five million pounds of public liability cover, a method
            statement and risk assessments, submitted early enough to be checked. Depending on the site it can also
            mean a partial or full closure while you fly, which carries a cost. Fine if the shot matters. Worth
            knowing before you promise it to a client.
          </p>
          <p class="src">
            <a href="https://cadw.gov.wales/visit/venue-hire/filming-photography/cadw-drone-policy" rel="noopener">Cadw — drone policy</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose-section" aria-labelledby="landowner">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 id="landowner">Permission to stand somewhere</h2>
        <div class="cols">
          <p>
            The one people forget. Every permission above is about the air. None of them gives anyone the right to
            stand on a piece of ground and launch from it.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a house shoot that is usually the owner saying yes, which they have already done by booking. For a
            field, a track or a verge it is whoever owns that field, track or verge. I sort it out before the day
            rather than knocking on a door with a drone in my hand.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section closing">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="closing__line">
          Tell me the postcode and I will tell you which of these applies before you commit to anything.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top:2rem"><a class="btn" href={url("/#enquiry")}>Send an enquiry</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .head h1 {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .prose-section .wrap {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.5rem 3rem;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    align-items: start;
  }

  @media (min-width: 56rem) {
    .prose-section .wrap {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(12rem, 16rem) minmax(0, 38rem);
    }
  }

  .prose-section h2 {
    max-width: 16rem;
  }

  .cols {
    max-width: 38rem;
  }

  .cols > * + * {
    margin-top: 1.1rem;
  }

  .cols p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .cols p {
    line-height: 1.62;
  }

  .cols strong {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .src {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.74rem;
    line-height: 1.9;
    color: var(--sea);
    padding-top: 0.9rem;
    border-top: var(--rule);
  }

  .tablewrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 2px solid var(--slate);
  }

  table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 40rem;
    font-size: 0.94rem;
  }

  th,
  td {
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    padding: 0.85rem 1.25rem 0.85rem 0;
    border-bottom: var(--rule);
    line-height: 1.45;
  }

  thead th {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--sea);
    font-weight: 400;
    padding-block: 0.75rem;
  }

  tbody th {
    font-weight: 500;
    width: 34%;
    padding-right: 2rem;
  }

  tbody td:last-child {
    white-space: nowrap;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
  }

  .closing__line {
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 60;
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    line-height: 1.2;
    letter-spacing: -0.018em;
    max-width: 32rem;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
</style>
